.TH UM80 1 "November 2025" "um80 0.3.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
um80 \- Microsoft MACRO-80 compatible assembler for 8080/Z80
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B um80
[\fB\-v\fR]
[\fB\-o\fR \fIoutput.rel\fR]
[\fB\-l\fR \fIlisting.prn\fR]
[\fB\-g\fR]
[\fB\-t\fR]
[\fB\-\-aseg\fR]
[\fB\-\-dri\fR]
[\fB\-D\fR \fISYMBOL\fR[=\fIVALUE\fR]]...
[\fB\-I\fR \fIPATH\fR]...
.I input.mac
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B um80
is an assembler that follows Microsoft MACRO-80 (M80).
um80 assembles 8080/Z80 source files (.MAC) and writes REL files (.REL).
The REL files are compatible with the
.BR ul80 (1)
linker.
.PP
um80 accepts the instruction sets of both the Intel 8080 and the Zilog Z80.
um80 also accepts macros, conditional assembly and the full set of the
directives of M80.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v\fR, \fB\-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.TP
.BI \-o " output.rel"
Specify the name of the output file.
The default is the name of the input file with a .rel extension.
.TP
.BI \-l " listing.prn"
Write a listing file.
The listing file shows the source, the code that um80 writes, and the
symbol table.
.RS
.PP
The linker has yet to finish some fields.
The listing file shows a field of this kind with the bytes that the REL file
carries:
.IP \(bu 2
For a relocatable word, the bytes are the offset of the word.
.IP \(bu 2
For a field that the linker computes or fills from an external symbol,
the bytes are 0.
.PP
um80 marks the field after its last byte, as M80 marks it:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB'\fR program relative
.IP \(bu 2
\fB"\fR data relative
.IP \(bu 2
\fB!\fR COMMON
.IP \(bu 2
\fB*\fR external
.RE
.TP
.B \-g\fR, \fB\-\-globals
Export all defined symbols as PUBLIC.
The option is useful to write complete symbol files for debugging.
The linker can then write all symbols with their final relocated addresses
if you use the option \fB\-S\fR.
.TP
.B \-t\fR, \fB\-\-truncate
Cut every PUBLIC, EXTRN, ## external and module name in the REL file to
6 characters, as M80 does.
.RS
.PP
Use the option when both conditions are true:
.IP \(bu 2
The linker links the REL file with REL files that M80 assembled.
.IP \(bu 2
A PUBLIC, EXTRN or ## name is longer than 6 characters.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-aseg
Start the file in ASEG, so that an ORG is an absolute address.
The rule is as in MAC, the assembler of Digital Research (DRI).
MAC has no relocatable segments.
.TP
.B \-\-dri
Read the source as MAC and RMAC read it where they differ from M80:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
um80 ignores a \fB$\fR inside a name, so NMB$LST and NMBLST are one symbol.
\fBPUBLIC A$BC\fR writes ABC.
Without \fB\-\-dri\fR, a \fB$\fR is part of the name, as in M80.
.IP \(bu 2
um80 keeps a \fB$\fR in these places:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
a \fB$\fR that starts a word (the location counter)
.IP \(bu 2
a \fB$\fR in a quoted string
.IP \(bu 2
a \fB$\fR in a comment
.IP \(bu 2
a \fB$\fR in the arguments of a macro call
.IP \(bu 2
a \fB$\fR in the list of an IRP or IRPC
.RE
.PP
MAC reads these arguments and lists as text.
.IP \(bu 2
um80 matches a macro body to its parameters with the names of MAC.
A name of MAC ends at a \fB.\fR, a \fB_\fR or a \fB$\fR.
For an \fB&\fR in a string, um80 uses the rules of RMAC.
Without \fB\-\-dri\fR, um80 reads a macro body as M80 does.
.IP \(bu 2
Only an argument that starts with \fB%\fR is a value.
A \fB<\fR or \fB>\fR in such an argument is an operator.
A \fB%\fR with nothing after it is an error.
M80 passes 0 in this case.
.IP \(bu 2
An item of an IRP list ends at a comma.
um80 skips a blank or a tab at the start of an item.
A blank or a tab anywhere else in the list is an error, and so is a \fB;\fR.
The rule for the list is as MAC and RMAC read it otherwise or flag it.
M80 ends an item at a comma, a \fB;\fR, a blank or a tab.
.IP \(bu 2
um80 reads the list of an IRP and the string of an IRPC as a macro argument.
The brackets of a group work wherever they are in the argument.
\fBIRP X,<1,2>3\fR is 1 and 23.
M80 ends the list at the \fB>\fR.
With \fB\-\-dri\fR, text after the list is an error.
.IP \(bu 2
The arguments of a macro call end at a blank after an argument.
Text after the blank, other than a comma, is an error.
M80 reads the blank as a comma.
.IP \(bu 2
A \fB"\fR in the arguments of a macro call or in an IRP list is text.
The character is not a quote.
.IP \(bu 2
An IF that a macro expansion leaves open ends with the macro expansion.
An IF that one repetition of a REPT, IRP or IRPC leaves open ends with
the repetition.
.IP \(bu 2
um80 defines a label on the ENDM that ends a body where the body ends.
.IP \(bu 2
An IRPC with an empty string repeats once.
.IP \(bu 2
The first word of a statement that is not an instruction, directive or
macro is a label, colon or not, in any column (\fBOBP DS 1\fR).
Without \fB\-\-dri\fR, a label needs its colon, as in M80.
.IP \(bu 2
um80 ignores a line number in front of a statement, a line that starts
with \fB*\fR and the assembly controls of MAC (\fB$\-MACRO\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
A \fB!\fR ends a comment and starts the next statement
(\fBNOP ;c! DB 1\fR is 00 01).
An ENDM after a \fB!\fR ends the macro body that um80 defines.
Without \fB\-\-dri\fR, a comment continues to the end of the line, as in M80.
.IP \(bu 2
After a macro call, the next statement starts after a \fB!\fR.
The \fB!\fR is the first \fB!\fR after the arguments that follows a
character other than a blank.
The rule is as in MAC.
\fBMM ;c! DB 1\fR assembles the DB.
\fBMM A;c! DB 1\fR, \fBMM A ! DB 1\fR and \fBMM ;c ! DB 1\fR omit
the DB, with a warning.
.IP \(bu 2
An 8AH (a line feed with bit 7 set) inside a line is a line feed.
In a string, the 8AH is the byte 0AH.
In the arguments of a macro call, the 8AH is text.
An 8DH is a carriage return that ends the statement.
um80 reads the next item as the line feed of the carriage return.
MAC reads an 8AH and an 8DH in this way.
M80 ignores an 8AH and ends the line at an 8DH.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPUSH A\fR and \fBPOP A\fR are PUSH PSW and POP PSW.
\fBMOV M,M\fR is 76H.
An expression can have two register names in it.
M80 flags each of \fBPUSH A\fR, \fBPOP A\fR, \fBMOV M,M\fR and an
expression with two register names.
um80 without \fB\-\-dri\fR also flags each of \fBPUSH A\fR, \fBPOP A\fR,
\fBMOV M,M\fR and an expression with two register names.
.IP \(bu 2
The relational operators \fB=\fR, \fB<\fR, \fB<=\fR, \fB>\fR, \fB>=\fR and
\fB<>\fR of MAC are EQ, LT, LE, GT, GE and NE.
M80 has no such operator.
.IP \(bu 2
HIGH and LOW apply to all that follows them (\fBHIGH(X)+1\fR is HIGH(X+1)),
as in MAC.
M80 applies HIGH and LOW to the term after them.
.IP \(bu 2
A label on an ORG is the location that the ORG sets, as in MAC.
M80 uses the location before the ORG.
The operand of the ORG can be a symbol that the source defines further down.
Then the label is an error, as MAC flags it P.
.IP \(bu 2
An END of a symbol that is not defined takes no start address and is no
error, as in MAC.
Example: \fBEND START\fR with START below the END.
M80 flags the END U.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMACLIB\fR \fIname\fR reads \fIname\fR.LIB and then \fIname\fR.MAC,
as in MAC and RMAC.
M80 reads \fIname\fR.MAC.
.IP \(bu 2
An END in a MACLIB library ends the library, as in MAC and RMAC.
M80 reads a MACLIB file as an INCLUDE file.
The END of an INCLUDE file ends the source.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-dri\fR does not imply \fB\-\-aseg\fR (the absolute sources of MAC) or
\fB\-t\fR (the six-character names of RMAC).
.IP \(bu 2
um80 accepts \fB'\fR only as the quote of a string, as in MAC.
\fBDB "A"\fR is an error.
M80 reads a string there.
.IP \(bu 2
A two-character string used as a value has its first character in the low
byte, as in MAC.
\fB'AB'\fR is 4241H, and \fBDW 'AB'\fR is 41 42.
M80 gives 4142H and 42 41.
.IP \(bu 2
An IF is true when bit 0 of its value is set, as in MAC.
\fBIF 2\fR and \fBIF NOT 1\fR are false.
M80 takes any value but 0 as true.
IFT, IFE, IFDEF and the other IFs that MAC does not have keep the meaning
of M80.
.IP \(bu 2
um80 reads a MACLIB library in pass 1 only, as MAC does.
um80 assembles none of the code or data of the library.
A symbol that the library defines keeps its value of pass 1.
The size of each segment and COMMON block is the most that pass 1 reached
in it.
The size counts the code and data of the library.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-D " SYMBOL" [= VALUE ]
Define a symbol before assembly begins.
If you give no value, the symbol has the value 1.
You can give the option more than once.
.TP
.BI \-I " PATH"
Add a directory to the include file search path.
You can give the option more than once.
.SH SOURCE FORMAT
A source file has lines that contain:
.IP \(bu 2
Labels (optional) followed by a colon.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A name is letters, digits and \fB$ . ? @ _\fR.
A digit does not start a name.
.IP \(bu 2
A name may begin with a dot (\fB.FOO:\fR, \fB..BAR\fR), as in M80.
.IP \(bu 2
A directive that starts with a dot (for example \fB.Z80\fR and
\fB.PHASE\fR), with a colon at once after the directive, is a label
(\fB.Z80:\fR changes no mode).
.IP \(bu 2
The first word of a statement is the name of an instruction, a directive or
a macro, in column 1 or not (\fBNOP\fR in column 1 is an instruction).
The exception is the name in front of an EQU, SET, DEFL, ASET or MACRO.
.IP \(bu 2
The first word of a statement can be no instruction, directive or macro
(\fBLAB: 5,6\fR).
Then um80 assembles the statement as DB, with a warning, as M80 does.
Thus a label without a colon is an error (see \fB\-\-dri\fR).
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Instructions or directives
.IP \(bu 2
Comments that start with a semicolon (;)
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
        ORG     0100H
START:  MVI     A,0         ; Initialize A
        LXI     H,BUFFER    ; Point to buffer
        CALL    INIT
        JMP     START
BUFFER: DS      100
        END     START
.fi
.RE
.SH DIRECTIVES
.SS Assembly Control
.TP
.B ORG \fIaddress\fR
Set the location counter to \fIaddress\fR.
.TP
.B END \fR[\fIaddress\fR]
End of source. The optional \fIaddress\fR specifies the entry point.
.SS Data Definition
.TP
.B DB \fIvalue\fR[,\fIvalue\fR]...
Define bytes. A value can be a number or a quoted string.
.TP
.B DW \fIvalue\fR[,\fIvalue\fR]...
Define 16-bit words (low byte first).
.RS
.PP
An empty operand of a DB or a DW is 0, as in M80, with a warning.
\fBDB 1,\fR is 01 00.
\fBDW\fR with no operand is 00 00.
With \fB\-\-dri\fR, an empty operand is an error, as MAC flags it.
.RE
.TP
.B DS \fIcount\fR
Define storage (reserve \fIcount\fR bytes).
.SS Symbol Definition
.TP
.B EQU \fIvalue\fR
Define a constant symbol.
You cannot redefine the symbol.
.TP
.B SET \fIvalue\fR
Define a variable symbol.
You can redefine the symbol with SET or DEFL.
.TP
.B DEFL \fIvalue\fR
Same as SET.
.SS Segments
.TP
.B CSEG
Select the code segment (program relative).
.TP
.B DSEG
Select the data segment (data relative).
.TP
.B ASEG
Select the absolute segment.
.PP
As in M80, a segment directive continues at the highest of these locations:
.IP \(bu 2
The locations that are set in the segment (by an ORG, or by the end of a DS).
.IP \(bu 2
The locations where the segment was left.
.PP
After an ORG back below an earlier ORG, the segment directive does not
continue where the segment was left.
.TP
.B COMMON \fR/\fIname\fR/
Select or define a COMMON block.
Each COMMON statement starts at the beginning of its block, as in M80
(the rule of FORTRAN).
.SS External Linkage
.TP
.B PUBLIC \fIsymbol\fR[,\fIsymbol\fR]...
Make symbols available to other modules.
.TP
.B EXTRN \fIsymbol\fR[,\fIsymbol\fR]...
Declare symbols defined in other modules.
.SS Macros
.TP
\fIname\fB MACRO \fR[\fIparam\fR[,\fIparam\fR]...]
Start a macro definition.
.TP
.B ENDM
End the macro definition.
.TP
.B LOCAL \fIsymbol\fR[,\fIsymbol\fR]...
Declare local symbols in a macro.
.RS
.PP
A LOCAL after a \fB!\fR declares local symbols too, as in MAC
(\fBNOP! LOCAL QQ\fR, \fBLOCAL QQ! NOP\fR).
The symbols are local for the statements after the LOCAL and for the lines
after the LOCAL.
In \fBLXI H,QQ! LOCAL QQ\fR, the LXI reads the QQ outside the macro.
.RE
.TP
.B EXITM
Stop the macro expansion before the end of the macro.
.TP
.B <\fItext\fB>
In a macro argument or an IRP item: \fItext\fR as it is, its commas and
semicolons too.
.RS
.PP
The brackets work wherever they are in the argument (\fBMM 1<2>3\fR passes
123, \fBMM <1,2>3\fR passes 1,23).
Only the outer brackets work (\fB<<A>>\fR is <A>).
The rules are as in M80, MAC and RMAC.
.RE
.TP
.B %\fIexpression\fR
As a macro argument: the value of the expression when the macro is called,
as digits in the current radix, written as M80 writes them (with
.B .RADIX 16,
26 is 1A and 160 is 0A0).
With
.B \-\-dri
only an argument that starts with % is a value, as in MAC.
.SS Repeat Blocks
.TP
.B REPT \fIcount\fR
Repeat the block \fIcount\fR times.
.TP
.B IRP \fIparam\fR,<\fIvalue\fR[,\fIvalue\fR]...>
Repeat the block for each value.
.TP
.B IRPC \fIparam\fR,\fIstring\fR
Repeat the block for each character of the string.
.SS Conditional Assembly
.TP
.B IF \fIexpression\fR
Assemble the code that follows when the expression is not zero.
.TP
.B ELSE
Assemble the code that follows when the IF was false.
.TP
.B ENDIF
End the conditional block.
.TP
.B IFDEF \fIsymbol\fR
Assemble the code that follows when the symbol is defined.
.TP
.B IFNDEF \fIsymbol\fR
Assemble the code that follows when the symbol is not defined.
.SS Listing Control
.TP
.B TITLE \fItext\fR
Set the listing title.
.TP
.B PAGE
Start a new listing page.
.TP
.B .LIST
Enable listing output.
.TP
.B .XLIST
Disable listing output.
.TP
.B .CREF\fR, \fB.XCREF
Cross-reference listing on and off, as in M80.
um80 writes no cross-reference file and assembles nothing for them.
.SS Processor Mode
.TP
.B .Z80
Enable the Z80 instruction set.
.TP
.B .8080
Accept only the 8080 instruction set (default).
.SS Include Files
.TP
.B INCLUDE \fIfilename\fR
Include another source file.
If the name has no extension, um80 reads \fIfilename\fR.MAC.
.TP
.B MACLIB \fIname\fR
Read a macro library.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Without \fB\-\-dri\fR, um80 reads \fIname\fR.MAC, as M80 does.
M80 reads the file as an INCLUDE file.
.IP \(bu 2
With \fB\-\-dri\fR, um80 reads \fIname\fR.LIB and then \fIname\fR.MAC,
as MAC and RMAC do.
.IP \(bu 2
If the name has an extension, um80 reads that file.
.RE
.PP
um80 searches for a file in this order:
.IP 1. 3
The directory of the source file.
.IP 2. 3
The current directory.
.IP 3. 3
Each \fB\-I\fR directory.
.PP
The search uses the name as written, and then the name in upper and in
lower case.
A CP/M file name has no letter case.
For example, the sources of DRI say \fBmaclib diskdef\fR for DISKDEF.LIB.
.SH EXPRESSIONS
Expressions can include:
.IP \(bu 2
Decimal numbers (default)
.IP \(bu 2
Hexadecimal numbers (suffix H or prefix 0x)
.IP \(bu 2
Octal numbers (suffix O or Q)
.IP \(bu 2
Binary numbers (suffix B)
.IP \(bu 2
ASCII characters in quotes ('A')
.IP \(bu 2
Symbols
.IP \(bu 2
Current location counter ($)
.PP
Operators: + \- * / MOD AND OR XOR NOT SHL SHR HIGH LOW
.PP
Without \fB\-\-dri\fR, as in M80 3.44:
.IP \(bu 2
A unary minus or plus applies to the term after it, before * / MOD SHL SHR.
\fB\-1 SHR 8\fR is 00FFH.
.IP \(bu 2
/ and MOD divide signed.
\fB8000H/2\fR is 0C000H.
The remainder has the sign of the quotient.
.PP
With \fB\-\-dri\fR, as in MAC:
.IP \(bu 2
The sign applies to all of these operators.
\fB\-1 SHR 8\fR is 0.
.IP \(bu 2
Division is unsigned.
\fB8000H/2\fR is 4000H.
.PP
With and without \fB\-\-dri\fR, \fIx\fR MOD 0 is \fIx\fR.
Without \fB\-\-dri\fR, \fIx\fR/0 is an error.
With \fB\-\-dri\fR, \fIx\fR/0 is 0FFFFH, as in MAC.
.PP
um80 passes some expressions to the linker as REL extension link items,
as M80 3.44 does.
An expression is of this kind when both conditions are true:
.IP \(bu 2
The value of the expression depends on where the linker places a segment,
or on an external symbol.
.IP \(bu 2
The expression is not simply an address plus a constant.
Examples are HIGH or LOW of a relocatable label, and any relocatable or
external value in a one-byte field.
.PP
AND, OR, XOR, SHL, SHR and the comparisons have no linker operator.
On such a value in an instruction, DB or DW, they are errors.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Assemble with default output:
.B um80 program.mac
.TP
Specify output file:
.B um80 \-o code.rel program.mac
.TP
Write a listing file:
.B um80 \-l program.prn program.mac
.TP
Define a symbol:
.B um80 \-D DEBUG=1 program.mac
.TP
Add an include path:
.B um80 \-I /usr/share/include program.mac
.TP
Export all symbols for debugging:
.B um80 \-g program.mac
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful assembly (the assembly can have warnings).
.TP
.B 1
Assembly failed due to errors.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I input.mac
Source file.
.TP
.I output.rel
REL file.
.TP
.I output.prn
Listing file (if you ask for it).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ul80 (1),
.BR ulib80 (1),
.BR ucref80 (1),
.BR ud80 (1)
.PP
The original Microsoft MACRO-80 manual is in the retro_docs archive,
https://github.com/avwohl/retro_docs/blob/main/um80_and_friends/m80.pdf
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.B um80
is compatible with Microsoft MACRO-80 (M80). These notes apply:
.IP \(bu 2
A source file can have Unix (LF) or DOS (CR/LF) line endings.
.IP \(bu 2
um80 converts symbol names to uppercase.
.IP \(bu 2
The REL format of um80 is binary-compatible with the REL format of M80.
LINK-80 (L80) 3.44 loads the REL files of um80.
.IP \(bu 2
L80 refuses a symbol of more than 7 characters.
For an external symbol inside a link-time expression, the limit is
6 characters.
um80 writes such a symbol in an extended form, and only ul80 reads the
extended form.
.IP \(bu 2
M80 itself writes the first 6 characters of a PUBLIC or external name in
the REL file, as
.B \-t
does.
.SH AUTHOR
um80 project contributors.
